    image: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576091160550-2173dba999ef?w=800&q=80",
    content: `<p>Do you experience jaw pain, headaches, or a clicking sound when you open your mouth? You may be suffering from a temporomandibular joint (TMJ) disorder. At <strong>Uplift Dental &amp; Orthodontics</strong> in <strong>Garden Grove, CA</strong>, we provide comprehensive <a href="/tmj-treatment/">TMJ treatment</a> to help patients find relief from this often-debilitating condition.</p><h2>What Is TMJ Disorder?</h2><p>TMJ disorder (also called TMD) refers to a group of conditions that affect the temporomandibular joints — the two joints on either side of your face that connect your jawbone to your skull. These joints, along with the surrounding muscles and ligaments, are responsible for the movements of your jaw that allow you to chew, speak, and yawn. When something goes wrong with this complex system, it can cause significant pain and dysfunction.</p><h2>Common Causes of TMJ Disorder</h2><p>TMJ disorder can develop from a variety of causes. Teeth grinding or clenching (bruxism), particularly during sleep, is one of the most common causes. Arthritis in the joint, injury or trauma to the jaw, stress that causes you to tighten facial and jaw muscles, misalignment of the teeth or bite, and disc displacement within the joint can all contribute to TMJ problems.</p><h2>Recognizing TMJ Symptoms</h2><p>TMJ disorder can manifest in many ways. Common symptoms include pain or tenderness in the jaw, especially when chewing, aching pain around the ear, difficulty or discomfort when opening or closing the mouth, clicking, popping, or grinding sounds in the jaw joint, facial pain or fatigue, headaches that may mimic migraines, neck and shoulder pain, and locking of the jaw in an open or closed position.</p><h2>TMJ Treatment Options</h2><p>Treatment for TMJ disorder depends on the underlying cause and severity of your symptoms. Our approach at Uplift Dental may include custom <a href="/night-guards/">night guards</a> or oral splints to prevent teeth grinding and reduce jaw strain, lifestyle modifications such as stress management and dietary changes, physical therapy exercises to strengthen and stretch jaw muscles, medications to manage pain and inflammation, and orthodontic treatment to correct bite alignment issues.</p><h2>When to See a Dentist for TMJ</h2><p>If you are experiencing persistent jaw pain, clicking, or any of the symptoms described above, do not ignore them. TMJ disorder rarely resolves on its own and often worsens without treatment. Early intervention leads to better outcomes and can prevent the condition from affecting your quality of life.</p><h2>Get TMJ Relief Today</h2><p>Stop living with jaw pain. <strong>Call Uplift Dental &amp; Orthodontics at <a href="tel:7148983308">${PRACTICE.phone.display}</a></strong> to schedule a TMJ evaluation at our <strong>${PRACTICE.address.city}, ${PRACTICE.address.state} ${PRACTICE.address.zip}</strong> office. We will identify the cause of your discomfort and create a personalized treatment plan to help you find lasting relief.</p>`,
